---
source: tests/steps/l01_05_lexer_strings/holes.rs
expression: "out.join(\"\\n\")"
---
"\"${name\""
    [7..8] this string is not closed before the end of the file. Write the closing `"` where its text ends.
"\"${x // c}\""
    [5..11] this `//` comment is inside a `${…}` hole of a `"` string, and a `//` comment runs to the end of the line, past the `}` that closes the hole. Write the comment as `/* … */`, or move it out of the string.
"\"${x\n}\""
    [1..4] this `${` hole of a `"` string reaches the end of the line without its closing `}`, and a `"` string, with its holes, is one line. Write the `}` on this line, or make the string a `"""` string, which may run over several lines.
"\"${\"inner}\""
    [1..11] this `${` opens a hole in a string, and the file ends before the `}` that closes it. Write `}` after the expression in the hole, or `\${` for the text `${` itself.
"\"${x /* open }\""
    [5..15] this block comment, opened with `/*`, is not closed before the end of the file. Write `*/` where the comment should end.
"\"\"\"\n  ${x}\n    \"\"\""
    [4..6] this line of a `"""` string is indented less than the closing `"""`, whose indentation is removed from every line of the string. Indent this line at least as far as the closing `"""`, or move the closing `"""` left.
